mastery.solution_text:
Reflect the decreasing branch in $y=x$. Its closed endpoint $(1,-1)$ becomes $(-1,1)$. The origin stays fixed, so both inverse intercepts are $(0,0)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.6cm,y=0.6cm,every node/.style={font=\normalsize}]
\draw[->] (-2,0)--(7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2)--(0,7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-2,-2)--(7,7);
\draw[thick,domain=-1.8:1,samples=80,<-] plot (\x,{(\x-1)*(\x-1)-1});
\draw[thick,domain=-1.8:1,samples=80,<-] plot ({(\x-1)*(\x-1)-1},\x);
\fill (1,-1) circle (1.4pt);
\fill (-1,1) circle (1.4pt);
\node[anchor=east] at (-2.6,1) {$(-1,1)$};
\draw[thin] (-2.5,1)--(-1.15,1);
\node[anchor=west,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] (originLabel) at (2.5,1) {$(0,0)$};
\draw[thin,shorten >=3pt] (0.15,0.06)--(originLabel.west);
\node[anchor=north,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] (inverseLabel) at (5,-2.5) {$f^{-1}$};
\draw[thin,shorten >=3pt] (5,{1-sqrt(6)})--(inverseLabel.north);
\node[left] at (-1.8,6.84) {$f$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
